Template: rcssl-vision-processor-gui/service
Type: select
Choices-C: none, boot, now
Choices: Don't run it as a service, Start it at boot, Start it at boot and now
Default: none
Description: Run the vision_processor GUI as a system service?
 As a service, the GUI runs in the background as the rcssl-vp-gui user and
 keeps its configuration in /var/lib/rcssl/vision-processor-gui.
 .
 Otherwise, run rcssl-vision-processor-gui yourself, or as your own user
 service: systemctl --user enable --now rcssl-vision-processor-gui
 .
 To change this later: sudo dpkg-reconfigure rcssl-vision-processor-gui

Template: rcssl-vision-processor-gui/listen
Type: select
Choices-C: local, network
Choices: This machine only, Any machine on the network
Default: local
Description: Who can open the GUI?
 The GUI has no login. Anyone who can reach it can change the running
 configuration and overwrite its files, including locked calibrations.
 .
 Choose the network only on a network you trust. The port is 8085, or the one
 in /etc/default/rcssl-vision-processor-gui if you've changed it there.
